Message-ID: <U_JoZ6LCYng.LocCt47LBwAJ@knitr.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/knitr/c/U_JoZ6LCYng/m/LocCt47LBwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Is there any way to show chunk options in the output document? I understand that chunk options are never shown because, like LaTeX commands, they are generally typesetting instructions that are not of interest to the reader. And if one does want to disclose this information, the .Rnw or .Rmd document can be shared.

That said, certain plotting instructions, such as fig.height and fig.width are of interest to someone who is interested in the plotting code, and seem to me to be on a different plane than other chunk options.  They are fundamental to the look of the plot and are really part of the R code rather than meta-code/display options, as it were.  In a call to a graphics device in an ordinary R script they are visible:

png("test.png", width = 480, height = 480, units = "px")

With knitr, I found a workaround to display the height and width with:

 x <- opts_current$get(c("fig.width","fig.height"))

cat(paste0("fig.width = ",x[[1]],", fig.height = ",x[[2]]))

I can shorten the code by creating functions to get and show fig options, but it is still awkward and requires in turn echo = c(-1,-2) so these lines aren't shown when I display the code.  Is there a simpler way to do this?

Thank you so much for knitr; it is a true paradigm shifter.

Joyce

